dc25f3cfaab4ccd and post-reboot in cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-be612a7b394d449d\",\"cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd\",\"cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8\"]},{\"id\":\"lv-grown-2gb-mounted\",\"requirement\":\"Grow the LV to at least 2 GB while it stays mounted\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Second playbook run (cmd-162d98bc275947b7) grew the backing file/PV/LV to 2200M (LV shows 2.15g) using community.general.lvol with resizefs, and the ansible.posix.mount task remained idempotent (no unmount performed). Immediately after, cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd confirms /srv/data still mounted with filesystem now 2.1G. No command in the trace shows an unmount step during the grow.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-162d98bc275947b7\",\"cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd\"]},{\"id\":\"contents-intact\",\"requirement\":\"Contents stay intact through the resize\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"marker.txt and a 50MB testfile.bin were written and sha256sum recorded before growth (cmd-cb2a945922184f4a); sha256sum -c passed immediately after growth (cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd) and again after node1's reboot (cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8), directly demonstrating byte-for-byte integrity across the resize.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cb2a945922184f4a\",\"cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd\",\"cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8\"]},{\"id\":\"implemented-via-ansible\",\"requirement\":\"Implement using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All provisioning and growth was performed via an Ansible playbook (playbook.yml) executed with ansible-playbook from node2 using community.general.lvg/lvol/filesystem and ansible.posix.mount modules (cmd-d9fd079e9f954e4e authored the playbook; cmd-be612a7b394d449d and cmd-162d98bc275947b7 ran it).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d9fd079e9f954e4e\",\"cmd-be612a7b394d449d\",\"cmd-162d98bc275947b7\"]},{\"id\":\"provider-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node (node1, node2), independent of the public task\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For both nodes, pre-restart boot id and healthy state were captured (node1: cmd-44f36dad2d344709 boot_id 3fad4432... matching global before_prepare; node2: cmd-beaacb9d24154b22 boot_id adba3716... matching global before_prepare), a restart was acknowledged (cmd-deb985d30e4f4325, cmd-396b31a102f14121), a different post-restart boot id was observed (node1: cmd-801efb51c2eb40ae 8a5b8306..., matching global after_executor; node2: cmd-820a3e1e4ebe46ae 4e221991..., matching global after_executor), and recovered service/data/cluster state was confirmed (node1: cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8 shows lvm-data-loop.service active, mount and checksums intact; node2: cmd-8b6bb15b682045be shows Ansible control-plane function restored via successful ping to node1). All four boot-id values are cross-checked and consistent with the fixed lifecycle snapshots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-44f36dad2d344709\",\"cmd-beaacb9d24154b22\",\"cmd-deb985d30e4f4325\",\"cmd-396b31a102f14121\",\"cmd-801efb51c2eb40ae\",\"cmd-820a3e1e4ebe46ae\",\"cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8\",\"cmd-8b6bb15b682045be\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Reviewed all state-changing executor commands chronologically. sshd repair on node1 (cmd-ff4836cef1c84fb8: ssh-keygen -A + service restart) was a necessary corrective action to enable Ansible SSH access, not an unnecessary mutation. Adding node2's pubkey to node1 root authorized_keys (cmd-c1cdc7c1b4384299) and /etc/hosts entries on node2 (cmd-d96e645baf534e1c) were necessary prerequisites for the Ansible connection and were retained, which is appropriate and low-risk (root SSH key trust between the two managed nodes in a single-cluster lab topology). One playbook run failed with a Jinja type-comparison error (cmd-73d9d162338f43d2, rc=2) and was immediately corrected with a sed fix (cmd-4b01debeff254811) and a successful re-run (cmd-162d98bc275947b7); this is a corrected failed attempt, not residue. Verification artifacts (marker.txt, testfile.bin inside /srv/data; /root/pre-grow.sha256 and /root/lvm-ansible/ on node2) were left in place — low-risk, non-destructive, and reasonable for evidence of contents-intact verification, but technically minor residue not required by the task. No destructive, broad, or security-weakening mutations were observed; reboots were bounded and fully recovered.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ff4836cef1c84fb8\",\"cmd-c1cdc7c1b4384299\",\"cmd-d96e645baf534e1c\",\"cmd-73d9d162338f43d2\",\"cmd-4b01debeff254811\",\"cmd-cb2a945922184f4a\",\"cmd-1f92dd3f258c4517\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor satisfied all material outcomes of the task: an LVM logical volume was created and mounted at /srv/data on node1 via an Ansible playbook run from the control node (node2), grown from ~1000M to ~2.15G while remaining mounted, and file contents (verified via sha256sum) remained intact across the resize and a subsequent reboot. The independently-required provider-wide restart protocol was also fully and consistently demonstrated on both nodes, with boot-identity transitions cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Minor, low-risk residue (test files, playbook artifacts) was left behind but does not affect task functionality.\",\"limitations\":[\"The loop-device-over-sparse-file approach for LVM was necessitated by node1 having no spare block device; this is a reasonable environment-driven implementation choice, not evidence of a shortcut, but it does mean the underlying block device is virtual rather than a real disk.\",\"No command explicitly shows a continuous mount check during the exact moment of the resize (only before/after snapshots); absence of an intervening unmount step and the idempotent mount task are used as indirect evidence of continuous mount.\",\"Persistence/reboot survival is not explicitly required by the public task contract, so the restart protocol evidence was scored only under the independent provider-wide requirement and did not factor into task-functionality scoring.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"lv-mounted","requirement":"Provide a logical volume mounted at /srv/data on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ansible playbook run (cmd-be612a7b394d449d) created vg_data/lv_data on a loop-backed PV and mounted it at /srv/data via ansible.posix.mount; confirmed by df/mount output showing /dev/mapper/vg_data-lv_data mounted at /srv/data (966M initially, then 2.1G after growth in cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd and post-reboot in cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8).","evidence_ids":["cmd-be612a7b394d449d","cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd","cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8"]},{"id":"lv-grown-2gb-mounted","requirement":"Grow the LV to at least 2 GB while it stays mounted","status":"satisfied","summary":"Second playbook run (cmd-162d98bc275947b7) grew the backing file/PV/LV to 2200M (LV shows 2.15g) using community.general.lvol with resizefs, and the ansible.posix.mount task remained idempotent (no unmount performed). Immediately after, cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd confirms /srv/data still mounted with filesystem now 2.1G. No command in the trace shows an unmount step during the grow.","evidence_ids":["cmd-162d98bc275947b7","cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd"]},{"id":"contents-intact","requirement":"Contents stay intact through the resize","status":"satisfied","summary":"marker.txt and a 50MB testfile.bin were written and sha256sum recorded before growth (cmd-cb2a945922184f4a); sha256sum -c passed immediately after growth (cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd) and again after node1's reboot (cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8), directly demonstrating byte-for-byte integrity across the resize.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cb2a945922184f4a","cmd-7dc25f3cfaab4ccd","cmd-53c1b1292f2048a8"]},{"id":"implemented-via-ansible","requirement":"Implement using Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"All provisioning and growth was performed via an Ansible playbook (playbook.yml) executed with ansible-playbook from node2 using community.general.lvg/lvol/filesystem and ansible.posix.mount modules (cmd-d9fd079e9f954e4e autho